WEBVTT

00:00.150 --> 00:11.555
Charon, formal designation (134340) Pluto I, is the largest of the five known natural satellites of the dwarf planet Pluto.

00:12.032 --> 00:17.694
It has a mean radius of 606 km (377 mi).

00:18.119 --> 00:27.169
Charon is a planetary-mass moon and the sixth-largest known trans-Neptunian object after Pluto, Eris, Haumea, Makemake, and Gonggong.

00:27.854 --> 00:41.197
It was discovered in 1978 at the United States Naval Observatory in Washington, D.C., using photographic plates taken at the United States Naval Observatory Flagstaff Station (NOFS).
